From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0252] fix(apps): the dispatched request stays the current one

Two corrections to the card 0251 opened, both found by the demonstration rather
than by reading it.

The demonstration has to move register bandwidth, not a rail.  0148 booted the
rail pair and the admission screen refused both halves before either cost a
token:

  [ERROR] SWEET APPS QUEUE: phase=refuse kind=rail-vote client=1 resource=0 detail=invalid result=0xe00002c2
  [ERROR] SWEET APPS QUEUE: phase=refuse kind=rail-vote client=1 resource=1 detail=invalid result=0xe00002c2
  [ERROR] SWEET APPS QUEUE: phase=selftest result=failed detail=pair-did-not-serialize

That is the screen working and the demonstration being wrong.  This boot's
display client votes every rail as a point, min == max, and the aggregate takes
the maximum of the floors and the minimum of the ceilings, so any second
consumer that raises a floor is illegal by the same rule that protects the
first one; the old code also cut its step at maxMV, which produced a vote equal
to the one already held and therefore a submission that never left the array.
Register bandwidth is the one quantity that can be moved legally here: it
aggregates by maximum, so asking for more is always accepted and always flies.
The pair now asks for one whole encoded step of CN0 twice - the step being one
unit of the y field scale() rounds to at the vote's own bus width, never a
percentage, because anything smaller rounds to the same y and moves nothing.
Both targets are proved before a token is spent, two fresh consumers are taken
so the two admissions cannot merge, and the row names the resource and both
byte rates so the demonstration can be read against the aggregate.

The second correction is a real hole in the dispatch path.  A request taken out
of the queue was never made current, so from the moment it started flying a
second submission for the same resource was screened only against the queue -
which no longer held it - and against fCurrent, which still described whatever
ran before.  The duplicate screen is what keeps one caller from stacking two
votes on one resource, and it must not weaken exactly when the path it protects
is the one being exercised.  dispatchNext() now makes the request it is about to
launch the current one, and launch() clears it again when the dispatch needed no
write, so a request that never reached the array does not hold the screen
against its successor.

The host evidence is the demonstration itself, extended by this patch: two
clients submit through the ordinary entry point on a boot that asked for the
property, the first dispatches directly under seq 0, the second is parked at
depth 1 and dispatched from the queue only after the first retired, and a
duplicate submission from the second client is refused with already-outstanding
both while it waits and while it flies.  A control boot without the property
runs one vote, dispatches it directly and leaves the queue empty with a
high-water mark of zero.

